WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:18.760
On October 15th 1997 Thrust SSC became the
first land vehicle to break the sound barrier,

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:24.740
breaking the land speed record with an astonishing
top speed of 1,228 km/h, which still stands

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:25.740
today.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.430
20 years on and the team that created this
wonder of engineering is now looking to break

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:33.980
their own record with the new and improved
Bloodhound SSC, a vehicle looking to achieve

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:41.410
an amazing milestone of 1,000 mph, or 1609
km/h for those of us living in the civilised

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:42.410
world.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:46.300
Creating a land vehicle capable of achieving
these speeds presents some very unique engineering

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:49.960
challenges, and today we are going to explore
a problem that even limits the top speed of

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:51.260
the Bugatti Chiron.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:57.579
How to design a wheel capable of withstanding
the extreme forces at these speeds.

00:00:57.579 --> 00:01:01.489
When designing a high speed car there are
two problems of physics that grow disproportionately

00:01:01.489 --> 00:01:02.920
the faster you travel.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:07.180
First, the drag force the vehicle experiences
grows with the square of the speed.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:12.210
Returns in top speed for every unit of horsepower
included shrinks the faster you go, but that’s

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:13.680
a problem for a future video.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.630
Today, we are going to explore the second
problem, the massive inertial forces the tyre

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:20.110
experiences at high speed.

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:23.990
If you watched my video on Artificial Gravity,
you will know that any spinning mass will

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:28.979
experience an increase in weight, proportional
to the square of the angular velocity multiplied

00:01:28.979 --> 00:01:31.409
by the distance from the rotational center.

00:01:31.409 --> 00:01:36.061
This phenomenon can be used to create artificial
gravity in space, but put too much mass on

00:01:36.061 --> 00:01:40.330
the outside of the space ship, or spin it
too fast and it could tear itself apart.

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:45.470
The Slow Mo Guys did an absolutely mesmerizing
test of this when they spun a CD up to 23,000

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:49.380
revolutions per minute, before it shattered
under the weight of it’s own inertia.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.420
This effect is one of the key limiting factors
currently holding cars like the Bugatti Chiron,

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:58.881
the Hennessey Venom and Koenigsegg Agera from
the illustrious milestone of 300 mph, just

00:01:58.881 --> 00:02:02.420
this month the Agera broke the production
speed record and came the closest to that

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:05.780
milestone with a top speed of 277.9 mph (447
km/h).

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:10.509
There isn’t a tyre on earth that can withstand
the inertial forces at higher speeds, the

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:14.620
rubber would simply peel away from the hub,
but evidently we aren’t far off.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:18.790
However these cars don’t come close to the
speeds of the land speed record held by Thrust

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:19.810
SSC.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:23.819
So what kind of wheel did the Thrust SSC use
when it demolished the land speed record all

00:02:23.819 --> 00:02:25.849
the way back in 1997?

00:02:25.849 --> 00:02:29.310
Well the Thrust SSC has one primary advantage
when it comes to wheels.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:33.510
It has very little use for traction beyond
braking and turning, as the wheels are not

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:37.920
used to transmit rotational motion from the
engine to linear motion, they achieve their

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.140
propulsion from jet engines and rockets.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:44.071
These wheels simply need to support the 7.5
tonne weight of the vehicle and allow it to

00:02:44.071 --> 00:02:48.100
roll along the ground, and have enough lateral
traction to allow the driver to steer the

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.100
car.

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:52.330
At higher speeds this even becomes unnecessary,
as the vehicle get’s the majority of its

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:55.480
steering force from the force of air hitting
the angled wheels.

00:02:55.480 --> 00:03:00.170
The Bloodhound derives it power from a EJ200
jet engine, the same engine used by the Eurofighter

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:03.790
Typhoon, (90 kN), and an even more powerful
hybrid rocket engine (122 kN).

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:08.410
At it’s fastest the wheels of the Bloodhound
will be rotating 10,000 times per minute.

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:13.230
Using that equation from earlier, and with
the wheel radius at 46.5 cm, we can calculate

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:17.880
that any mass on the outside of the rim will
experience 50,000 times the acceleration due

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:18.880
to gravity.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:24.010
A 1 kilogram bag of sugar would weigh the
same as a fully laden articulated truck.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:29.260
With these problems in mind, let’s begin
the design process for our 1000 mph wheels.

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:34.260
Step one is material selection, this will
determine a large portion of our design process.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:38.680
As the design will change according to material
properties and manufacturing techniques.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:43.159
As explained before traction is not a huge
concern, so we can forgo the rubber tyre and

00:03:43.159 --> 00:03:47.080
instead go with a solid metal wheel, which
can better withstand the centrifugal force

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:48.640
caused by the spinning wheel.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:52.440
The metal needs to not only be strong enough
to withstand these forces, but it also needs

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:55.269
to be light to minimise this inertial force.

00:03:55.269 --> 00:03:59.740
On top of all this, the material needs to
be capable of absorbing damage, which is why

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:04.180
a carbon fibre wheel is not an option, as
at these speeds an unexpected hit from a stone

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:08.349
could potentially shatter the entire wheel,
if the material is too brittle.

00:04:08.349 --> 00:04:12.920
These are a very particular set of requirements,
that forged aerospace grade aluminium fulfills

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:13.920
best.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.060
Now that the material has been selected we
can begin forging blanks.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:21.940
To forge these wheels the team took these
huge billets of aluminium alloy 7037 and heated

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:26.970
them to 390 degrees and compressed it with
a 3,600 tonne forging press.

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:31.750
The forging clamp operator here deserves credit
for the insane precision, taking this cylindrical

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:36.509
billet and forming it into a compressed disk,
this is our blank, which will be passed to

00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:39.610
a milling machine to mill the wheel into it’s
final shape.

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:43.669
Transforming the cast material, which is material
that was formed by pouring molten aluminum

00:04:43.669 --> 00:04:48.300
into a cast, into this forged material makes
the material vastly stronger.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:52.260
When the molten aluminium is cooling to become
solid, the crystal structure grows randomly

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:56.150
from nucleation sites, like a snowflake from
a single ice crystal.

00:04:56.150 --> 00:05:00.400
This unpredictable process gives rise to a
random jumble of crystal sizes, grain directions

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:04.290
and voids, called dislocations, between individual
crystal grains.

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:08.500
When this solidified cast aluminium is compressed
the crystal grains increase in density and

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:13.669
dislocations pile up, which increases the
energy required to cause expansive deformation.

00:05:13.669 --> 00:05:17.350
This process is called work hardening, and
it drastically increases the strength of the

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:22.039
material, but also makes it less ductile,
but that’s desirable in this situation,

00:05:22.039 --> 00:05:24.850
as we do not want the wheel expanding during
use.

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:28.810
Now that we have our blank material, we can
begin the prototyping process.

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:32.620
We do not want to use this expensive material
and begin prototyping with it.

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:35.070
Failed designs would be extremely expensive.

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:38.419
The team will first start with some basic
design parameters, like wheel diameter.

00:05:38.419 --> 00:05:42.919
They will then determine the general thickness
needed for vehicle stability and to withstand

00:05:42.919 --> 00:05:44.410
the predicted stress.

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:48.110
Using these parameters a model will be generated
and tested computationally.

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:52.560
We performed our own computational analysis
on stress and deformation from inertial forces

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:55.110
on the Bloodhound wheel using simscale here.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:58.280
Using a tool like this, the engineers will
refine the design.

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:02.500
Once a suitable design is found, prototyping
with a cheaper material will begin.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:07.259
Many engineering firms today use 3D printing
for this design verification step, but the

00:06:07.259 --> 00:06:09.770
Bloodhound team used a cheap cast aluminium
wheel.

00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:13.300
They attached this wheel to a trailer that
simulated the weight of the bloodhound on

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:17.310
a single axle, and it helped them discover
that their wheel did not adequately spread

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:21.419
the load over the desert surface, meaning
it was breaking through the crust and driving

00:06:21.419 --> 00:06:24.780
on the hard bedrock underneath, which would
damage the wheel.

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:28.069
Back to the drawing board to increase the
contact surface of the wheel.

00:06:28.069 --> 00:06:32.419
This design and prototyping process will be
repeated until a suitable design is found.

00:06:32.419 --> 00:06:37.250
Once the final design was decided on the expensive
forged aluminium was passed to a CNC milling

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:41.490
machine, which uses computer guidance to cut
the wheel to it’s final shape.

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:45.300
However, when a work hardened material is
machined, it disrupts the compressive forces

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:48.139
that developed in the skin during the forging
process.

00:06:48.139 --> 00:06:52.210
The surface of the machined metal is like
a bottle of compressed air, and a small leak

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:53.940
may lead to an explosion.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:58.380
It wants to expand, so if a crack forms in
the surface of this material, these expansive

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:02.860
forces will increase the chances of the crack
growing, and so another surface treatment,

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:07.300
called shot peening, is applied by shooting
thousands of tiny spherical balls at the surface

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:11.419
of the machined product to introduce a thin
layer of higher compression, which helps the

00:07:11.419 --> 00:07:13.300
material resist crack growth.

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:17.710
This final product was sent for design verification
again, where it was rotated up to speed in

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:22.410
a controlled environments to ensure it would
safety take this astounding vehicle to 1.3

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:24.120
times the speed of sound.

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:29.090
Just two months ago the Bloodhound SSC completed
a 200 mph test run at Cornwall airport.

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:34.699
The Bloodhound reached this top speed in just
8 seconds, with a 0-60 of just 2 seconds.

00:07:34.699 --> 00:07:37.370
That half a second faster that the Bugatti
Chiron.

00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:41.410
This run served to test the Eurofighter Typhoon
engine and to demonstrate the vehicle to the

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:45.419
public, after all the goal is not to break
the record, they already have the record with

00:07:45.419 --> 00:07:46.900
the Thrust SSC.

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:50.900
The real goal here is to inspire people to
get excited about engineering, which I can

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:53.229
